    <body>
      <sec id="article-145887.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>The ABO blood group system is crucial for safe blood transfusions, classified into 4 types&#x02014;A, B, AB, and O&#x02014;based on the presence or absence of antigens A and B on red blood cells and corresponding antibodies in serum. To determine blood types, clinicians perform forward and reverse groupings by identifying ABO antigens on red blood cells and corresponding&#x000a0;antibodies in serum. Forward grouping identifies antigens on red blood cells using anti-A and anti-B antisera, whereas reverse grouping identifies antibodies using prepared red cells to confirm the results. ABO discrepancies occur when forward and reverse grouping results do not align due to unexpected or missing reactions. These discrepancies can result from technical errors, weak antigens or antibodies, autoantibodies, alloantibodies, or abnormal proteins.</p>
        <p>Experts categorize ABO discrepancies into 4 types&#x02014;type I due to weak or missing antibodies, type II due to weak or missing antigens, type III due plasma or protein abnormalities leading to&#x000a0;pseudo-agglutination, and type IV due to miscellaneous causes leading to false reactions. Proper resolution of these discrepancies is vital, as incorrect blood typing can lead to fatal hemolytic reactions. Thorough serological evaluations and patient history assessments are essential for maintaining transfusion safety. This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying and managing ABO discrepancies. Clinicians gain a deeper understanding of the implications and management of ABO discrepancies along with a heightened awareness of interprofessional collaboration for timely evaluation and management, leading to enhanced patient outcomes and reduced morbidity and mortality.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the causes and classifications of ABO discrepancies to ensure accurate blood typing and transfusion safety.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based strategies to resolve blood grouping discrepancies.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select the correct transfusion approach based on resolved ABO typing results.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Communicate effectively with laboratory personnel and transfusion medicine specialists regarding complex ABO discrepancies.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=145887&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=145887">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145887.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>ABO discrepancies pose a significant challenge in clinical practice, necessitating a thorough understanding of blood group typing to ensure transfusion safety.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145887.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145887.r2">[2]</xref> The ABO system, crucial in blood banking, categorizes blood into 4 primary types&#x02014;A, B, AB, and O&#x02014;based on the presence or absence of antigens on red blood cells (RBCs). Laboratory personnel perform forward grouping by detecting antigens on the RBC surface, whereas reverse grouping confirms results by identifying antibodies in the patient's serum. Discrepancies arise when these results do not align, potentially due to technical errors, weak antigen or antibody expression, underlying medical conditions, or the presence of subgroups or abnormal proteins. Depending on the discrepancy, up to 10% of patients tested may show weak or missing antibodies or antigens or even pan-agglutination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145887.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;The presence of weak red cell antigens can prompt a delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145887.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Experts classify ABO discrepancies into 4 major types&#x02014;type I due to weak or missing antibodies,&#x000a0;type II due to weak or missing antigens,&#x000a0;type III due plasma or protein abnormalities leading to&#x000a0;pseudo-agglutination, and&#x000a0;type IV due to miscellaneous causes leading to false reactions. These inconsistencies can lead to potentially life-threatening hemolytic reactions if not accurately identified and resolved.</p>
        <p>Clinicians must collaborate with laboratory specialists to conduct thorough serological investigations, including extended incubation, adsorption, and elution techniques, and review patient histories for recent transfusions or medical conditions that may impact blood typing. Standardized protocols, strict quality control measures, and interdisciplinary communication are vital in mitigating the risks associated with ABO discrepancies.&#x000a0;Effectively managing ABO discrepancies is crucial for transfusion safety, minimizing adverse reactions, and enhancing patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145887.s3" sec-type="Function">
        <title>Function</title>
        <p>Specific antigens on the surface of RBCs and the corresponding antibodies in serum determine the 4 main blood types. These blood groups are inherited based on the <italic toggle="yes">ABO</italic>&#x000a0;gene (OMIM 616093), which encodes enzymes that modify carbohydrate molecules on RBCs. The backbone carbohydrate, the H antigen, encoded by&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">FUT1 </italic>(OMIM 211100), is a&#x000a0;fucosyltransferase to which the sugars <italic toggle="yes">N</italic>-acetylgalactosamine for the A antigen and d-galactose for the B antigen are added.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145887.r5">[5]</xref> More specifically, the A antigen occurs on RBCs when the enzyme&#x000a0;&#x003b1;-1,3-<italic toggle="yes">N</italic>-acetyl-D-galactosyltransferase adds&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">N</italic>-acetylgalactosamine to the H antigen.&#x000a0;The B allele of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">ABO</italic>&#x000a0;encodes for the glycosyltransferase enzyme that adds d-galactose to the H antigen, creating the B antigen on the RBC surface.&#x000a0;The O allele encodes for a nonfunctional enzyme, leaving the H antigen unmodified.</p>
        <p>Individuals&#x000a0;with type A blood have A antigens on the RBC surface and anti-B antibodies, whereas those with type B blood have B antigens and anti-A antibodies. Individuals&#x000a0;with type AB blood possess both A and B antigens without anti-A or anti-B antibodies, making them universal plasma donors and recipients of RBCs. Individuals with type O blood lack A and B antigens but have anti-A and anti-B antibodies, making them universal RBC donors but restricted recipients.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>An additional component of blood type is the Rh blood group system. The Rh antigens, located on proteins in the RBC membrane and encoded by&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">RHD&#x000a0;</italic>(OMIM 111690) and&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">RHCE&#x000a0;</italic>(OMIM 617970&#x000a0;), further refine blood compatibility. The most common Rh antigens include D, C, c, E, and e. Individuals whose RBCs lack the RhD antigen are classified as Rh-negative</p>
        <p>Unlike most antibodies, anti-A and anti-B antibodies develop naturally without prior exposure to blood products. As the gut becomes colonized in early infancy, infants<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>between 4&#x000a0;and 6 months develop antibodies against bacterial antigens that closely resemble A and B antigens through a process known as molecular mimicry. In contrast, antibodies to Rh antigens typically develop only after exposure to foreign RBCs through transfusion or pregnancy.</p>
        <p>Clinicians perform blood group typing or ABO typing with both forward and reverse typing, whereas Rh testing only involves forward-type testing.&#x000a0;The forward grouping reaction, which detects the antigen on the surface of the patient's RBCs, should match the results of the reverse grouping reaction, which detects the antibodies in the patient's serum. Naturally occurring anti-A and anti-B antibodies should be present in individuals lacking the corresponding RBC antigens.&#x000a0;Clinicians expect the forward and reverse grouping reactions to be strong (level 3+ or 4+) (See <bold>Table 1.&#x000a0;</bold>Expected ABO Typing Results). In&#x000a0;cases of ABO discrepancies, weak reactions (level 1+ or 2+) occur.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145887.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-145887.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1.&#x000a0;Expected ABO Typing Results</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="2">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 26px;">
                <td style="width: 47px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Blood Group</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Forward Reaction&#x000a0;(patient's RBCs with reagent antibodies)</bold>
<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 74px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 201.115px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Reverse Reaction (patient's serum with reagent RBCs)</bold>
</td>
